{
  "id": "childhood-vaccination",
  "title": "Reaching zero-dose children, by country",
  "summary": "Outreach to find and vaccinate \"zero-dose\" children, who got no routine vaccines, in the 20 countries where most of them live. Sized from WHO/UNICEF's 2025 national immunization coverage estimates (WUENIC): zero-dose counts, DTP3 and measles coverage. Program costs are illustrative assumptions.",
  "valueUnit": "zero-dose children reached per $1,000 (lens-weighted)",
  "sources": [
    {
      "name": "WHO/UNICEF Estimates of National Immunization Coverage (WUENIC), 2025 revision — WHO Immunization Data portal",
      "url": "https://immunizationdata.who.int/"
    },
    {
      "name": "WHO — WUENIC overview and methods",
      "url": "https://www.who.int/teams/immunization-vaccines-and-biologicals/immunization-analysis-and-insights/global-monitoring/immunization-coverage/who-unicef-estimates-of-national-immunization-coverage"
    },
    {
      "name": "Immunization Economics — Ethiopia study of delivery cost per zero-dose child reached (2025)",
      "url": "https://immunizationeconomics.org/recent-activity/2025/11/26/ethiopia-study-finds-delivery-cost-per-zero-dose-child-reached-as-low-as-6-65/"
    }
  ],
  "assumptions": {
    "cost_per_child_first_usd": 50,
    "hardest_to_reach_cost_multiple": 2.5,
    "reach_fraction_per_round": 0.1,
    "reach_scale_exponent": 0.25,
    "need_weight_exponent": 2,
    "note": "Illustrative program economics, not quoted costs. (1) The first zero-dose children a program reaches cost about $50 each and the hardest-to-reach cost 2.5x that (about $125); this brackets the $52-$129 per zero-dose child reached that a 2025 Ethiopia costing study (Afar and Somali regions) found for routine outreach and mobile health teams, though costs vary widely by setting and strategy. (2) Each country's cap is the budget to reach 10% of its 2025 zero-dose children at the first-child cost of $50; because later children cost more, a fully funded round reaches somewhat fewer. (3) Value per dollar falls along each curve as the easiest children are found first, with most of the drop late in the round. (4) Lens weights are recommender judgments, not data: Reach scales value by (zero-dose count / median count)^0.25, on the view that large concentrations of unreached children let fixed outreach costs go further; Coverage gap scales by (DTP3 gap / mean DTP3 gap)^2; Outbreak risk scales by (MCV1 gap / mean MCV1 gap)^2, where a gap is 100% minus coverage. (5) Countries are the 20 with the most zero-dose children in WUENIC 2025; coverage is WUENIC's national estimate, rounded to whole percent."
  },
